<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pouvanaa a Oopa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pouvanaa Oopa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pouvanaa a Oopa was a Tahitian nationalist politician and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> veteran who led the movement for Polynesian rights after 1945. He sat in the French National Assembly from 1949 and headed the territorial government, but campaigned for a 'no' vote in the 1958 referendum; arrested later that year, he was convicted of plotting to burn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a> and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He returned in 1968, became a senator, and his conviction was annulled in 2018.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pouvanaa a Oopa was born on 10 May 1895 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeva">Maeva</a>, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a>, to a Polynesian mother and a Danish sailor. He served in the Pacific Battalion of the French army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and worked as a carpenter and a fried-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> vendor. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> he criticised those who profited from the war and in 1942 was exiled to a reef islet of his native Huahine. After the war he went on attacking French colonial rule, and in 1947 he was prosecuted for challenging government authority but acquitted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1947 he founded the Democratic Rally of the Tahitian People (RDPT), which called for an end to French colonial rule. Elected at the 1949 by-election that followed the death of Georges Ahnne, he became the first French Polynesian to sit in the French Chamber of Deputies, and he was re-elected in 1951 and 1956. Under the slogan &quot;Tahiti for the Tahitians; Frenchmen into the sea!&quot; the RDPT swept local elections, and his government brought in the territory's first income tax, repealed after a strike by business leaders and a riot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a>.</p><p>In the 1958 referendum officials restricted the campaign for 'no', and in some outlying islands voters did not know it was an option; 'no' lost by 62 to 36 per cent. Charged with arson, he was found guilty in October 1959 and sentenced to eight years in prison and fifteen years of exile in metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He was released from prison in 1962 and pardoned by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> in 1968. In 1971 he was elected to the French Senate, where he sat until his death in Tahiti on 10 January 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He is regarded as the metua, the father, of French Polynesia's independence movement. A monument to him was raised in 1982 before the Assembly of French Polynesia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papeete">Papeete</a>, and in July 1995 nearly a third of Tahiti's adults gathered there to protest against French nuclear tests in the Tuamotu Archipelago. His family's request for a new trial was refused in 1988; the Assembly called for one in 2009 and again in 2013. In 2018 the Court of Revision quashed his conviction after new evidence showed that police had fabricated evidence or extracted it by threats of violence, and that the Governor had reported his arrest before the fires were even set.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pouvanaa a Oopa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The nationalist leader <b>Pouvanaa a Oopa</b>, who campaigned for independence, was arrested in 1958 and exiled after a disputed conviction.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-polynesia">French Polynesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huahine">Huahine</a>. Died in Tahiti. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-16.73,-151.03&amp;year=1948" aria-label="Huahine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="440 2785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pouvanaa a Oopa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M890 3055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M890 3055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-16.73,-151.03&amp;year=1948">What was here in 1948? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
